# Commits git blame should look straight through.
#
# The root .gitattributes handles generated output per *path*. This file handles
# the case that remains: a one-off commit that rewrote a pile of lines without
# changing any behavior — a repo-wide reformat, or a mass re-bundle after an
# esbuild or tooling bump that rewrites all 14 hook payloads at once. Blame on
# those lines otherwise names whoever ran the tool.
#
# Belongs here:
#   - the full 40-character SHA of the *landed* commit (the squash on main, not
#     the branch head that was pushed), one per line
#   - a `# <commit subject>` comment on the line above it
#
# Does NOT belong here: any commit that changed behavior, even slightly. A
# commit listed here is invisible to blame forever; that is only safe when the
# commit genuinely carries no authorship worth finding.
#
# GitHub reads this file from the repo root automatically. Local git does not —
# `blame.ignoreRevsFile` is set by scripts/setup.sh and by worktree
# provisioning. Note that git fails *fatally* when that config points at a
# missing file, so the setup scripts only set it when this file is present. To
# opt out locally: git config --unset blame.ignoreRevsFile
#
# Seeded empty on purpose — there is no such commit yet.
